+---
+title: "Use a dedicated replica for Materialize"
+description: "How to stand up a dedicated PostgreSQL replica via native logical replication and point a Materialize PostgreSQL source at it"
+menu:
+  main:
+    parent: "postgresql"
+    name: "Dedicated replica"
+    identifier: "pg-dedicated-replica"
+    weight: 60
+---
+
+This page shows you how to stand up a dedicated PostgreSQL replica using
+PostgreSQL's native [logical
+replication](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/logical-replication.html),
+and point a [PostgreSQL source](/sql/create-source/postgres/) at the replica
+instead of your production primary.
+
+Running Materialize against a dedicated replica isolates the load of change
+data capture (CDC) from your production primary: the primary only streams
+changes to the replica, and Materialize reads from the replica.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- You need a PostgreSQL 11+ primary with logical replication enabled
+  (`wal_level = logical`). For details, see the [self-hosted PostgreSQL
+guide](/ingest-data/postgres/self-hosted/).
+- You need a second PostgreSQL instance to serve as the dedicated replica. It
+  must be able to connect to the primary over the network.
+
+## A. Create a publication on the primary
+
+On the **primary**, create a publication for the tables you want to replicate.
+
+```postgres
+CREATE PUBLICATION mz_publication FOR TABLE table_1, table_2;
+```
+
+You can also publish all tables in the database:
+
+```postgres
+CREATE PUBLICATION mz_publication FOR ALL TABLES;
+```
+
+## B. Create a subscription on the replica
+
+On the **dedicated replica**, create a subscription that consumes the
+publication from the primary. This uses PostgreSQL's native logical
+replication to keep the replica's tables up to date.
+
+```postgres
+CREATE SUBSCRIPTION mz_subscription
+  CONNECTION 'host=<primary_host> port=5432 dbname=<dbname> user=<replication_user> password=<password>'
+  PUBLICATION mz_publication;
+```
+
+The replica must already contain the tables included in the publication (for
+example, created via `pg_dump --schema-only` from the primary).
+
+## C. Configure the replica for Materialize
+
+Materialize reads from the replica using PostgreSQL's logical replication
+protocol, so the replica must also support logical replication:
+
+1. Set `wal_level = logical` on the replica and restart it.
+
+1. Create a publication on the replica for the tables Materialize should
+   ingest:
+
+    ```postgres
+    CREATE PUBLICATION mz_source FOR TABLE table_1, table_2;
+    ```
+
+1. Create a replication user on the replica with the `REPLICATION` and `LOGIN`
+   privileges, and grant it read access to the published tables.
+
+## D. Point Materialize at the replica
+
+Create a [connection](/sql/create-connection/#postgresql) that targets the
+**replica** instead of the primary, and then create a source using that
+connection.
+
+```mzsql
+CREATE SECRET pg_replica_password AS '<password>';
+
+CREATE CONNECTION pg_replica TO POSTGRES (
+  HOST '<replica_host>',
+  PORT 5432,
+  DATABASE '<dbname>',
+  USER '<replication_user>',
+  PASSWORD SECRET pg_replica_password
+);
+
+CREATE SOURCE mz_source
+  FROM POSTGRES CONNECTION pg_replica (PUBLICATION 'mz_source')
+  FOR ALL TABLES;
+```
+
+For more details on the source syntax and options, see the [`CREATE
+SOURCE`](/sql/create-source/postgres/) reference.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- **Do not write to the replica.** The replica is a read-only target for
+  Materialize. Writes, schema changes, or DDL on the replica are not
+  propagated back to the primary and can break replication.
+- **Do not use the replica for failover.** This replica is dedicated to
+  Materialize and is not a standby for high availability. Use a separate
+  physical standby if you need failover.
+- **Schema changes** must be applied on the primary and will flow to the
+  replica through the subscription. After a schema change, you may need to
+  [handle the change in Materialize](/sql/create-source/postgres/#handling-errors-and-schema-changes).
+- **Replication slots** are created on the replica by Materialize. Monitor
+  them as described in [PostgreSQL replication
+  slots](/sql/create-source/postgres/#postgresql-replication-slots).
+
